Economic and environmental issues in materials selection — MIT 3.080 (@mat-selection-mit: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_36KMERFT8SDRP30MT63KQ5WS6C
Randolph Kirchain, MIT 3.080, Fall 2005; material-selection lectures by guest Jeremy Gregory. [Course and lecture sequence](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-080-economic-environmental-issues-in-materials-selection-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/), [published assignments](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-080-economic-environmental-issues-in-materials-selection-fall-2005/pages/assignments/), [projects](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-080-economic-environmental-issues-in-materials-selection-fall-2005/pages/projects/), [exams](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-080-economic-environmental-issues-in-materials-selection-fall-2005/pages/exams/).

Work the complete public sequence: engineering economics, selection of material/shape/process, cost modeling and life-cycle assessment. Start with selection lectures 11–13; return to economics and costs when comparing production choices. The assignment page supplies some homework, not every classroom exercise: EE1–3, CM1 with an injection-molding spreadsheet, and LC1. Lecture 5 has no posted uncertainty notes. Required commercial texts and software are not supplied by these links; use the other open readings and explicitly derived calculations where access is missing.

Indexes, assignment availability and selected selection-lecture derivations were inspected on 30 October 2026. Spreadsheet execution and every solution were not independently verified. MIT OCW uses CC BY-NC-SA with component exceptions. Preserve credits and keep course files external. Historical prices, software and industrial examples need their dates.

